  <title>Virtual machines</title>
  <p>A virtual machine is a brick that QEMU runs. Its settings have a
  section for each part of it: <gui>Machine</gui>, the QEMU program, the
  type of machine, its processors and its memory; <gui>Disks</gui>;
  <gui>CD-ROM and boot</gui>; <gui>Network</gui>; <gui>Display</gui>;
  <gui>Sound and USB</gui>; <gui>Kernel</gui>, to boot a kernel of your own;
  and <gui>Advanced</gui>. The line at the top says which QEMU will run
  it.</p>
  <figure>
    <title>The settings of a virtual machine</title>
    <media type="image" mime="image/png" src="figures/machine-settings.png">
      <p>The settings of vm1: the line at the top names its QEMU, the sections
      are at the left, and the section Machine shows Program, Machine type,
      CPU model, KVM, Virtual CPUs and Memory.</p>
    </media>
  </figure>
  <section id="disks">
    <title>Disks</title>
    <p><gui>Add Disk</gui> adds a disk on the first free device. Each disk
    chooses its image in a list of the images of the project, which says what
    each is and who uses it. The list also has <gui>Add an Existing
    Image…</gui>, <gui>New Empty Disk…</gui> and <gui>Manage Images…</gui>,
    which shows the <gui>Images</gui> tab.</p>
    <terms>
      <title>How a disk uses its image</title>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Private copy</gui></title>
        <p>The default. The machine writes its changes to a file of its own in
        the folder of the project, made at its next start, as
        <file>vm1_hda.cow</file>, and the image stays as it is. Several
        machines can share one image this way.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>The image itself</gui></title>
        <p>The machine writes to the image.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
    <p>The line under a disk says what it does. The menu of a disk has
    <gui>Save as a New Image…</gui>, which keeps the changes of the machine as
    an image of their own; <gui>Merge into</gui> the image, which writes them
    into it, and lists first what else uses it; <gui>Start Over from</gui> the
    image, which throws the changes away; <gui>Show in Files</gui> and
    <gui>Remove Disk</gui>. Saving and merging run in the background, with
    their progress, and can be stopped.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="network">
    <title>Network cards</title>
    <p><gui>Add Card</gui> adds a network card. The cards are in the order the
    machine sees them, the first one first. Each has a <gui>Model</gui>, a
    <gui>MAC address</gui>, and what it is <gui>Plugged into</gui>:</p>
    <list>
      <item><p>a switch of the lab;</p></item>
      <item><p><gui>The host only, on QEMU's user network</gui>: QEMU joins the
      machine to this computer, and through it to the networks this computer
      reaches, with no switch;</p></item>
      <item><p><gui>Other bricks plug into it</gui>: the card is a socket,
      and a wire can plug into it when the setting <gui>Plugs into
      machines</gui> of the project allows it;</p></item>
      <item><p><gui>Nothing</gui>.</p></item>
    </list>
  </section>
  <section id="console">
    <title>The console of a machine</title>
    <p>While the machine runs, <guiseq><gui>Process</gui><gui>Open Control
    Monitor</gui></guiseq>, in its menu, opens the monitor of QEMU in a
    terminal, the one of the <gui>Terminal</gui> setting. There you type the
    commands of QEMU, as <cmd>info network</cmd>.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="state">
    <title>Stop, suspend, resume</title>
    <p><gui>Stop</gui> asks the system of the machine to shut down.
    <gui>Suspend</gui>, under <gui>Process</gui>, saves the state of the
    machine in its first disk and stops it; <gui>Resume</gui> starts it again
    from there. <gui>Reset</gui> is the reset button of the machine.</p>
  </section>
